      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        The Thundering Herd Catastrophe in Enterprise Webhook Dispatchers
      </h2>
      <p>
        In modern event-driven architectures (such as Stripe, Shopify, GitHub, or internal microservice dispatchers), webhook dispatchers concurrently deliver tens of thousands of HTTP payloads per second. When an external customer endpoint or downstream API server suffers a transient 15-second outage—whether due to database lock contention, cold starts, or autoscaling delays—thousands of in-flight HTTP requests fail simultaneously with status codes like <code>500 Internal Server Error</code>, <code>502 Bad Gateway</code>, <code>503 Service Unavailable</code>, or <code>504 Gateway Timeout</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your background worker fleet applies standard deterministic exponential backoff (e.g. <code>t = base * 2^attempt</code>), a catastrophic harmonic resonance occurs:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Attempt 1 (t = 2s)</strong>: 10,000 failed requests sleep for exactly 2.0 seconds and wake up at the exact same millisecond, blasting the recovering endpoint with a synchronized 10,000 QPS spike.</li>
        <li><strong>Attempt 2 (t = 4s)</strong>: Having crashed the server again, all 10,000 requests fail in unison, sleep for exactly 4.0 seconds, and hit the endpoint simultaneously once more.</li>
        <li><strong>Cascading Failure</strong>: Even after the destination database has recovered, the customer server cannot exit its crashed state because your synchronized retry waves act as an unintentional distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This phenomenon is formally termed the <strong>Thundering Herd Problem</strong>. Eliminating it requires introducing statistical entropy into retry scheduling.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Mathematical Proof: Comparing the 4 Jitter Algorithms
      </h2>
      <p>
        In seminal research conducted by Marc Brooker at Amazon Web Services (AWS Architecture Blog), four mathematical backoff strategies were modeled under severe system contention. Let $base$ be the initial delay, $cap$ be the maximum allowed delay, and $n$ be the current retry attempt count:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-700 bg-slate-900/40 rounded-lg">
