      </p>

      <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 my-6">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-6">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-1">Base Delay (Seconds)</label>
            <input 
              type="number" 
              id="sim-base" 
              value="1.0" 
              step="0.5" 
              min="0.5" 
              class="w-full bg-slate-950 border border-slate-700 rounded p-2 text-sm text-white font-mono focus:border-cyan-500 focus:outline-none"
            />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-1">Maximum Delay Cap (Seconds)</label>
            <input 
              type="number" 
              id="sim-cap" 
              value="64.0" 
              step="8" 
              min="10" 
              class="w-full bg-slate-950 border border-slate-700 rounded p-2 text-sm text-white font-mono focus:border-cyan-500 focus:outline-none"
            />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-1">Total Retry Attempts</label>
            <select id="sim-attempts" class="w-full bg-slate-950 border border-slate-700 rounded p-2 text-sm text-white font-mono focus:border-cyan-500 focus:outline-none">
              <option value="4">4 Attempts</option>
              <option value="6" selected>6 Attempts</option>
              <option value="8">8 Attempts</option>
            </select>
          </div>
        </div>

        <button 
          id="sim-run-btn" 
          class="w-full py-2 bg-cyan-600 hover:bg-cyan-500 text-slate-950 font-bold font-mono text-sm rounded transition mb-6"
        >
          Generate Randomized Retry Intervals
        </button>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs font-mono border-collapse border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 rounded">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-900 text-cyan-400 border-b border-slate-800">
                <th class="p-2">Attempt</th>
                <th class="p-2">No Jitter (Deterministic)</th>
                <th class="p-2">Full Jitter Sample A</th>
                <th class="p-2">Full Jitter Sample B</th>
                <th class="p-2">Collision Probability</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody id="sim-results-body" class="divide-y divide-slate-800 text-slate-300">
              <!-- Populated dynamically via JS -->
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <script is:inline>
        function runSimulation() {
          const base = parseFloat(document.getElementById('sim-base').value) || 1.0;
          const cap = parseFloat(document.getElementById('sim-cap').value) || 64.0;
          const attempts = parseInt(document.getElementById('sim-attempts').value, 10) || 6;
          const tbody = document.getElementById('sim-results-body');
          tbody.innerHTML = '';

          for (let i = 1; i <= attempts; i++) {
            const exponentialCeiling = Math.min(cap, base * Math.pow(2, i));
            const noJitter = exponentialCeiling.toFixed(2) + "s";
            const sampleA = (Math.random() * exponentialCeiling).toFixed(2) + "s";
            const sampleB = (Math.random() * exponentialCeiling).toFixed(2) + "s";
            const collisionProb = (100 / (exponentialCeiling * 10)).toFixed(1) + "%";

            const tr = document.createElement('tr');
            tr.className = "hover:bg-slate-900/50";
            tr.innerHTML = `
              <td class="p-2 font-bold text-white">#${i}</td>
              <td class="p-2 text-rose-400">${noJitter}</td>
              <td class="p-2 text-emerald-400">${sampleA}</td>
              <td class="p-2 text-cyan-400">${sampleB}</td>
              <td class="p-2 text-slate-400">${collisionProb}</td>
            `;
            tbody.appendChild(tr);
          }
        }

        document.getElementById('sim-run-btn').addEventListener('click', runSimulation);
        document.getElementById('sim-base').addEventListener('change', runSimulation);
        document.getElementById('sim-cap').addEventListener('change', runSimulation);
        document.getElementById('sim-attempts').addEventListener('change', runSimulation);
        runSimulation();
      </script>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Queue Architecture Topologies: Delay Queues, Dead Letter Queues, and Poison Pill Isolation
      </h2>
      <p>
        Executing in-process thread sleep (<code>setTimeout</code> or <code>time.Sleep</code>) inside stateless webhook worker nodes is strictly an anti-pattern at enterprise scale. When workers sleep in RAM, process restarts drop retry timers, and memory leaks multiply under sustained outages.
      </p>
      <p>
        The production topology leverages external message broker delayed messaging:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Multi-Tier Delayed Queues (AWS SQS or RabbitMQ Dead Letter Exchange)</strong>: Instead of blocking workers, dispatchers publish failed messages to scheduled delay queues with the calculated Full Jitter delay encoded in the message delivery timer (<code>DelaySeconds</code>). The worker immediately acknowledges the message and returns to the pool.</li>
        <li><strong>Poison Pill Circuit Breakers</strong>: When a destination host repeatedly returns HTTP 400 or HTTP 401 (malformed authentication), retry attempts should be aborted immediately. Continuing to retry known invalid client configurations wastes system bandwidth.</li>
        <li><strong>Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) Offloading &amp; Replay Tooling</strong>: Upon exhausting 8 attempts over approximately 2 to 4 hours of total jittered time, the payload is persisted into a durable Cold DLQ (e.g. SQS DLQ or PostgreSQL table). An automated webhook event dashboard alerts the subscriber, providing an administrative endpoint for manual single-click re-dispatch once their endpoint is restored.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        In summary: Combining <strong>Full Jitter</strong> with idempotent payloads and decoupled message broker delay queues flattens peak network traffic by over 80%, protects downstream customer services from cascading outages, and establishes an enterprise-grade webhook delivery architecture in 2026.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
